    "lawCode": "Amd §1404, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3499A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to including credit\nunion shares, share certificates and share draft accounts as\nauthorized reserve investments for non-life insurers\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo authorize non-life insurers, such as property/casualty insurance\ncompanies, to purchase shares, share certificates and share draft\naccounts by state and federally-chartered credit unions.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nInsurance Law section 1404(a) is amended by adding a new paragraph 11\nto clarify that credit union shares, share certificates and share\ndraft accounts are expressly included within the list of authorized\ninvestments for non-life insurers.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn this period of unprecedented financial turmoil when even seemingly\n\"safe\" investments such as bonds are decreasing in value, it seems\nincongruous to not permit non-life insurers to invest in\nfederally-backed share certificates of credit unions. Yet since credit\nunion share certificates are considered \"equity\" instruments rather\nthan debt instruments such as a bank certificate of deposit, the\ncurrent Insurance Law is interpreted as not allowing investment by\nnon-life insurers in these federally-guaranteed share certificates.\nAlthough the Insurance Department has issued an opinion disallowing\nsuch investment based on the equity/debt distinction between credit\nunion share certificates and bank certificates of deposit, the\nDepartment in 2007 also found that an insurance agent or broker may\nmaintain a premium account in a federally-insured credit union.\nAlthough it is true that different sections of law and a different\ninstrument are involved, the broader policy for permitting both types\nof investments remains the same, namely the safety and security of a\nfederally-insured credit union. Additionally, in some upstate\ncommunities where some of these smaller insurers operate there may be\na limited number of available banking services and this measure will\nsimply afford them an additional and convenient option. This measure\nwould provide small and large insurance companies with another safe\navenue for investing their precious assets, one that is guaranteed by\nthe Federal government.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately after it shall have become a\nlaw.\n",
